# DTF tier manifest: auth-db-outage  (artifact-keeper#4750 / #4751, #4756, #4765)
# Profile-set: filesystem / single. The bug is in the credential check that
# runs ahead of every handler, so the storage dimension is irrelevant.
#
# THE BUG
# When PostgreSQL could not answer while a PRESENTED credential was being
# checked, the backend answered 401, as if the credential were wrong. Only a
# pool-ACQUIRE timeout (#2125) was mapped to 503; a refused / lost connection,
# a closed pool or a server shutting down fell through to the "invalid
# credential" branch:
#   * Bearer JWT on /api/v1: the live-user / credential-watermark lookup error
#     was ignored, the token fell through to the API-token lookup, and the
#     request got 401 "Invalid or expired token".
#   * Basic on a format route (password, or an AK JWT as the password): 401
#     "Authentication required".
#   * the /v2 OCI registry and its /v2/token exchange: 401 for every failure.
# Docker, twine, cargo and CI runners abort or re-prompt on 401 but retry on
# 503, so a DB blip (failover, restart) turned into hard CI failures. The fix
# (#4751) adds `AppError::is_db_unavailable()` and answers a retryable 503
# (`Retry-After: 1` on the middleware and /v2 paths, OCI code `UNAVAILABLE`).
# Nothing is ever authenticated on an error: it fails closed.
#
# THE FOLLOW-UPS (1.11.2)
# #4756: login itself, and any handler whose OWN query hit the outage (an API
# token is validated from the auth cache, so /api/v1/auth/me got past the
# middleware and failed in the handler), still answered 500 DATABASE_ERROR.
# Fixed by #4764: the central AppError mapping answers an outage-class
# database error with 503 DATABASE_UNAVAILABLE ("Database is temporarily
# unavailable, retry shortly") + Retry-After: 1.
# #4765: format handlers that build their own protocol error body (npm
# {"error"}, Git LFS {"message", "request_id"}, OCI, Connect, Swift, Maven
# metadata, Pub) shed only a pool timeout to 503; an outage was a
# non-retryable 500. Fixed by #4769: the same classification, 503 + Retry-After
# in each format's own envelope.
#
# THE PRIMITIVE: A REAL OUTAGE
# The oracle stops this slot's own Postgres container (`docker stop
# "$DB_CONTAINER"`, i.e. ak-dtf<slot>-db), probes, then starts it again. That is
# safe: DB_CONTAINER is exported by run.sh from the slot number, every DTF slot
# owns a separate compose project with its own Postgres, so no other slot or
# host stack is touched. An EXIT trap restarts the container if the oracle
# dies mid-outage, so `run.sh` teardown (and `--keep`) find a whole stack. Each
# outage probe waits for the pool's acquire timeout (5s default), so the
# stopped window is about a minute.
#
# REACHING A FORMAT HANDLER DURING THE OUTAGE (#4765)
# A password, a JWT, or an API token inside Basic is checked against the
# database by the auth middleware, which answers the #4751 503 before any
# handler runs; an anonymous LFS batch is refused outright (#508). The probes
# therefore present the user's API token as Bearer (validated from the auth
# cache, no query) on PUBLIC npm and gitlfs repos whose keys the oracle warms
# into the visibility middleware's 60 s repo cache immediately before the
# stop, and they run first in the outage. The format handler's own repository
# lookup is then the first query to fail.
#
# Oracle (discriminating, RELEASE_GATE=1):
#   (CONTROL)  true on BOTH images: before the outage a user's JWT, API token,
#              Basic password and JWT-as-Basic-password all authenticate, and
#              /v2/ accepts Basic; /readyz goes not-ready (503, database
#              unhealthy) once the DB is stopped; a credential rejected before
#              the database is consulted (a too-short X-API-Key) still gets 401
#              during the outage; after `docker start` /readyz returns to
#              ready, the pool reconnects (regression guard for the #1877
#              keepalive work), login works again, the SAME pre-outage JWT and
#              API token are still valid, a wrong password is 401 again (on
#              /maven and on login), and the npm GET / LFS batch answer
#              404 / 200 as they did before the outage.
#   (FIX)      during the outage each presented credential gets 503, never
#              401 and never 2xx: Bearer JWT on /api/v1 (+ Retry-After: 1);
#              Basic password, JWT-as-Basic-password and a WRONG Basic password
#              on a /maven route (the check cannot run, so "cannot decide");
#              /v2/ with a Bearer JWT and with Basic, and /v2/token with Basic
#              (503 UNAVAILABLE + Retry-After: 1). RED on 1.11.1: 401 on all.
#   (FIX #4756) during the outage, POST /api/v1/auth/login with the correct
#              AND with a wrong password, and a Bearer API token on
#              /api/v1/auth/me, answer 503 {code: DATABASE_UNAVAILABLE} +
#              Retry-After: 1 (and login mints no token). RED on 1.11.1 and
#              on main 6a4e29f1: 500 DATABASE_ERROR, no Retry-After.
#   (FIX #4765) during the outage a Git LFS batch answers 503 + Retry-After:
#              1 in the LFS envelope ({message: "Database is temporarily
#              unavailable, retry shortly", request_id}), and an npm metadata
#              GET answers 503 DATABASE_UNAVAILABLE + Retry-After: 1. RED on
#              1.11.1 and 6a4e29f1: 500 "Database operation failed".
#
# NOT GATED HERE: npm's own {"error"} envelope. Every npm read resolves the
# repository first through map_db_err, i.e. the generic AppError body (#4764),
# so with the whole database down the npm envelope's DB sites (after the
# repo lookup) are unreachable; the npm probe gates the 503 on the npm route,
# not the envelope. OCI's envelope is likewise behind the /v2 credential
# check (already 503 UNAVAILABLE); Connect, Swift, Maven metadata and Pub are
# not probed.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false so a 429 can never masquerade as either verdict.
PROFILES="storage.filesystem"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
